  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.7,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R7"],
      "pins": ["R7.1", "R7.2"],
      "nets": ["DE/RE node (U1.2/U1.3, Q2 collector)", "GND"],
      "observed": "On the schematic sheet, R7 (the pull resistor on the MAX3485 DE//RE enable node, tied between Q2's collector / U1 pins 2-3 and GND) is drawn with the value '20M'.",
      "expected": "The specification's component table lists R7 as 20 kΩ ('R6, R7, R8, R15 | 2k, 20k, 2.2k, 2.2k'). A 20 kΩ pull-down is needed to define the DE//RE state when Q2 is off; 20 MΩ is ~1000× too large and leaves the enable node effectively floating.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "R7 20M (resistor below Q2 BC857 collector, connected to the U1 pin2/pin3 DE//RE line)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "R6, R7, R8, R15 | 2k, 20k, 2.2k, 2.2k | Bias/pull network on the MAX3485 A/B differential lines and DE/RE enable line."}
      ],
      "impact": "With R7 at 20 MΩ the DE//RE node is not reliably pulled to its idle level when Q2 is non-conducting, so the MAX3485 direction control can float. In serial-servo mode the transceiver may fail to enable reception (or latch in one state), breaking the half-duplex auto-direction switching the design relies on.",
      "confidence": 0.7
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The board matches the specification in topology, part identities and most values, but R7 is drawn as 20 MΩ where the spec calls for 20 kΩ, leaving the MAX3485 DE//RE enable node essentially unpulled and jeopardising the auto TX/RX direction control used in serial-servo mode."
}

